# Local CTF working material. Each of these has ZERO commits on any branch
# of this repository, so nothing here has ever been intended as content -
# while `git add -A` would commit all of it, including a 19 MB
# tools/ghidra-dist/ghidra.zip, two 4 MB binaries, a tarball of this repo,
# and challenge flag.txt files. An archive in the history can only be
# removed by rewriting it, so the default is to leave it out; delete the
# line if a directory becomes content.
#
# `knowledge/` is deliberately NOT here: it is committed on branch ctf-v3,
# so the author does keep it.
# ...and ANCHORED, every one of them. A pattern without a leading slash
# matches a directory of that name at ANY depth, so bare `tools/` matched
# `argus/tools/` - the whole tool layer: registry.py, probes.py,
# analysis.py, web.py, team.py, mcp_tools.py, _subproc.py, __init__.py.
# Those eight are tracked, so they stayed in the repo, and nothing broke.
# The damage was prospective and silent: a NEW module under argus/tools/
# is refused by `git add`, skipped by `git add -A`, and not listed by
# `git status` either - while AGENTS.md tells a contributor that every tool
# must be registered in `tools/registry.py`. A registry importing a module
# that was never committed breaks a fresh clone and the wheel, and says
# nothing at commit time. `/*.tar.gz` below was already anchored; these
# were not. The conventional patterns at the top of this file are left
# unanchored on purpose - `selftest.py` now refuses ANY ignored tracked
# file and any ignored new path under the package, so a future collision
# of that kind is caught by the gate rather than by a broken clone.
/challenges/
/cache/
/solved/
/tools/
/*.tar.gz

# Baseline experiment output (docs/EXPERIMENTS.md writes exp/single-$i,
# exp/team-$i). Session dirs are runtime output, same as sessions/ above -
# the numbers go in the write-up, the transcripts stay local.
exp/
